;; A method whose name is a C# keyword, called across a module boundary.
;;
;; `ref` is the case — `Refable`'s method, and the one a stopwatch is read with
;; — but nothing about it is special: `sanitizeIdent` escapes a reserved word to
;; `@ref` so that C# reads it as an identifier at all.
;;
;; It escaped the *declaration* and not the *call*. A call to an imported impl
;; is emitted from a path, `Refable_Dial.Instance::ref`, and the escape was
;; applied to the whole string rather than to its segments — which matches none
;; of the reserved words the moment a dot is in it. The result was `.ref(...)`
;; against a method declared `@ref`: C# read `ref d` as a by-reference local,
;; and the errors landed in generated code, naming neither trait nor method.
;;
;; Three things had to line up, which is why it sat here unnoticed:
;;
;;   - the method's name is a reserved word, so the two sides must agree on an
;;     escape rather than on nothing;
;;   - the implementor is *not* generic, since the generic branch splits on
;;     `::` and sanitizes the halves separately and was always right;
;;   - the impl is in *another module*, since one written here is inlined at the
;;     call and never goes through a member access at all.
;;
;; The standard library's own `Refable` impls are generic — `Vec`, `Map`,
;; `Array` — so the suite exercised only the branch that worked. `(std
;; stopwatch)` implements it for `System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch`, which is where
;; this actually turned up: `(ref sw 'ms)` at the REPL.

(import (std prelude))
(import (std simpletest))
(import "inc/keyword_method.bjo")

(defun (main args)
  (def d (dial 21))

  (expect "a keyword-named method, called across a dll" (ref d 'setting) 21)
  (expect "and again, so it is the call and not the value" (ref d 'doubled) 42)

  ;; `try-ref` is the trait's default. It calls `ref` from inside prelude rather
  ;; than from here, so it is a second emission site for the same name.
  (expect "the trait default reaches it too" (try-ref d 'setting) (Some 21))

  ;; The generic implementors, which went through the other branch and were
  ;; always fine. Here so that a fix cannot quietly break them.
  (expect "ref on a vec" (ref [10 20 30] 1) 20)
  (expect "ref on a map" (ref (list->map (list (Tuple "a" 1))) "a") 1)
  (expect "ref on a string" (ref "abc" (string-cursor-start "abc")) #\a)
  0)
